# Lesson 4, new exercise: lead toward the king (K3 opposite 874) in a trump contract; it scores if West has the ace.
title: Lead toward the king
id: L04-10
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.4
student: N
board: 64
dealer: W
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  AKJ86  K3     J75    K94
  E  T5     QT2    QT98   QJT3
  S  Q742   874    AK3    A65
  W  93     AJ965  642    872

auction:
  W Pass
  N 1S     = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 15 points and five spades: open your long suit at the one level.
  E Pass
  S 3S     = 4+ spades, an opening hand or better: game forcing
  W Pass
  N 4S     = Game
     not *: Partner's 3♠ is forcing to game. Bid it.
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner jumped to 3♠, showing four spades and an opening
  hand or better, so you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid spades
  first, and so East will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 0 = Nine spades with the ace, king and queen.
  hearts: 2 = The ace, and your small heart. The king is a loser too, unless West has the ace and you lead toward the king.
  diamonds: 1 = Your third diamond.
  clubs: 1 = Your third club.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: When can your ♥K win a trick? Which hand should lead hearts?
play:
  1   E DQ  S DA  W D2  N D5
      note: Four losers, one too many. The ♥K can win a trick if West has the ace and you lead hearts from dummy, so West has to play before your king. Win in dummy.
  2   S SQ  W S3  N S6  E S5
      note: Draw trumps. Dummy's queen first, the high card from the short side.
  3   S S2  W S9  N SA  E ST
      note: Over to your ace. Both opponents followed twice, so trumps are drawn.
  4   N DJ  E D8  S DK  W D4
      note: Now get back to dummy, to lead a heart toward your king.
  5   S H4  W H5  N HK*  E H2
      note: Lead a low heart from dummy.
      HK key: West played low, so play the king. If West has the ace, the king wins.
      HK not H3: Did you think about the king? West has already played, so if West has the ace, your king wins now. Holding it back gains nothing.
  6   N H3  E HT  S H7  W HJ
      note: The king won. Your other heart is a loser: get it out of the way.
  7   W D6  N D7  E D9  S D3
  8   E CQ  S CA  W C2  N C4
  9   S C5  W C7  N CK  E C3
      note: Cash the ♣K.
  10  N C9  E CJ  S C6  W C8
      note: Your last club is a loser. Let it go.
  11  E DT  S S4  W H6  N S8
  12  N SK  E HQ  S S7  W H9
      note: The rest are yours.
  13  N SJ  E CT  S H8  W HA
      note: 10 tricks: 4♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many, and saw that the ♥K could
  win a trick if West held the ace and you led hearts from dummy.

  Leading toward an honor works because the opponent in front of it has to
  play first. If West plays the ace, your king is good later; if West plays
  low, the king wins now. Leading the king from your own hand would never
  work: the ace would simply take it. If East had held the ace, you would
  have lost two hearts and gone down one.
